 "public asByteArray ^<ByteArray> = (\n			(* Concatenate the parts to a String then char-code-extract into the\n	   ByteArray. Bytes > 255 are silently truncated by Uint8Array. *)\n			\n			| str = asString. result = ByteArray new: str size. |\n			1 to: str size do: [:i | result at: i put: (str at: i) ].\n			^result\n		)",
 "public addByte: byte <Integer> = (\n			(* Add the character whose code point is [byte] to the buffer.\n\n	   The previous implementation did [_js operator: '+' with: '' and: byte],\n	   which in JS produces the BYTE'S DECIMAL STRING (`'' + 70` is `'70'`,\n	   not `'F'`). That silently corrupted every caller — most visibly the\n	   compiler's [NameMangler>>_mangleSelector:] (which calls [addByte: c]\n	   for each character of a selector to build the JS-side mangled\n	   identifier). At deploy-time on psoup the same code worked because\n	   psoup's StringBuilder uses a ByteArray + copyStringFrom: to convert.\n	   At runtime in JS-deployed land, mangling [Foo`Bar] produced\n	   `$70111111$6697114` instead of `$Foo$Bar`, breaking every reflective\n	   lookup that re-mangles a name (find:in:, etc.).\n\n	   Use [String.fromCharCode(byte)] to actually convert the byte to its\n	   1-character string. *)\n			\n			sizeX:: sizeX + 1.\n			^parts add: (_js call: (_js propertyOf: (_js ident: 'String')\n						at: (_js literal: 'fromCharCode'))\n					with: {byte. })\n		)",
